WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.567 --> 00:00:01.601 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm very happy
to have you here.

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:02.936 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm really grateful that
you invited me.

00:00:02.936 --> 00:00:04.170 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Now before you were
a senator...

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.271 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes.
-...you were a mayor.

00:00:05.271 --> 00:00:06.439 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I was.
-And I would imagine that

00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:07.707 align:start size:94% position:6%
once you become a senator,
all of a sudden,

00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:10.243
the breadth of what you need
to know gets so much larger.

00:00:10.243 --> 00:00:11.911 line:92%
For example, a mayor of Newark

00:00:11.911 --> 00:00:13.246 align:start size:94% position:6%
doesn't need to know
foreign policy.

00:00:13.246 --> 00:00:16.249
-I had a lot of problems with my
International House of Pancakes.

00:00:16.249 --> 00:00:17.951 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, exactly.
-[ Chuckles ] I did. I did.

00:00:17.951 --> 00:00:19.486 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And so what was it like
when you first had

00:00:19.486 --> 00:00:22.088 align:start size:94% position:6%
to sort of undertake
the learning about things

00:00:22.088 --> 00:00:24.290 align:start size:94% position:6%
that are so important?
-I mean, you --

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:26.793
Even before I got in the office,
I called around

00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:30.497 align:start size:94% position:6%
from literally active generals
to foreign policy experts.

00:00:30.497 --> 00:00:31.765 align:start size:94% position:6%
But it's never like
the first time

00:00:31.765 --> 00:00:33.566 align:start size:94% position:6%
you have to deal with
a real issue.

00:00:33.566 --> 00:00:34.401 size:81% position:41% line:92%
And I'll never forget,

00:00:34.401 --> 00:00:37.804 align:start size:94% position:6%
the first time I was invited
to the situation room,

00:00:37.804 --> 00:00:39.472 align:start size:94% position:6%
it was the --
Vice President Biden

00:00:39.472 --> 00:00:41.574 size:81% position:41%
was meeting with us.
And I was all awkward.

00:00:41.574 --> 00:00:43.109 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think it was my first days,
literally, there.

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:45.678
And I'm excited this is --
I still can't believe I'm here.

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:47.847
-Yeah, you're a senator now.
You're in the situation room.

00:00:47.847 --> 00:00:49.182 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's a big deal.
-It is a big deal.

00:00:49.182 --> 00:00:51.451 align:start size:94% position:6%
My mom always says,
"behind every successful child

00:00:51.451 --> 00:00:53.219
is an astonished parent." And --
-Yeah.

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:54.754
[ Laughter ]
-And this is one of the moments

00:00:54.754 --> 00:00:56.222 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm astonished.
I'm -- I'm sitting there,

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:59.059 size:81% position:41%
and the Vice President
is ridiculously late,

00:00:59.059 --> 00:01:01.628
and we're all just sitting there
awkwardly -- awkwardly.

00:01:01.628 --> 00:01:03.596 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it was one of
the best moments for me

00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:04.764 line:92%
because there's Al Franken,

00:01:04.764 --> 00:01:06.366 align:start size:94% position:6%
and one of our other
Senate colleagues asked him,

00:01:06.366 --> 00:01:09.235
"Well, who was ever the worst
guest on 'Saturday Night Live?'"

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:11.071 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
And before you know it,

00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:13.606 align:start size:94% position:6%
here I am sort of awkward,
sitting there,

00:01:13.606 --> 00:01:14.240 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
intense and ready.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:17.677
And Al Franken, who is one of
the more brilliant senators

00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:18.845 line:92%
in the United States Senate.

00:01:18.845 --> 00:01:21.314 align:start size:94% position:6%
He's incredibly talented,
gifted.

00:01:21.314 --> 00:01:23.016 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:23.016 --> 00:01:24.918 align:start size:94% position:6%
-But he -- but he's also
wickedly funny.

00:01:24.918 --> 00:01:26.853 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah. He likes to tell
a story, too.

00:01:26.853 --> 00:01:27.754 size:84% position:42% line:92%
-He had us -- He had me

00:01:27.754 --> 00:01:29.155 align:start size:94% position:6%
literally fighting back
the tears,

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:30.723 align:start size:94% position:6%
laughing so hard
as I'm sitting there.

00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:32.725
And then the Vice President
comes in, and we get going.

00:01:32.725 --> 00:01:36.162 align:start size:94% position:6%
But look, it's -- it is,
regardless of what you think

00:01:36.162 --> 00:01:38.331 align:start size:94% position:6%
about partisan politics,
whatever,

00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:41.668
walking on to that Senate floor,
especially for me,

00:01:41.668 --> 00:01:43.403 line:92%
most Americans don't know,

00:01:43.403 --> 00:01:44.637 align:start size:94% position:6%
we haven't had that many
African-Americans

00:01:44.637 --> 00:01:47.407
that have served in the Senate.
I'm the fourth African-American

00:01:47.407 --> 00:01:48.842
popularly elected into
the United States Senate.

00:01:48.842 --> 00:01:50.276 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Wow.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:54.681
-So I never -- I never lose my
sense of gratitude to New Jersey

00:01:54.681 --> 00:01:57.016 align:start size:94% position:6%
nor my sense of history
of what it means

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:59.119
to walk on that floor every day
that I do.

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:00.286 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You obviously
walk on that floor

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:02.722 align:start size:94% position:6%
with colleagues on both sides
of the aisle.

00:02:02.722 --> 00:02:03.756 align:start size:94% position:6%
We mentioned earlier
in the show,

00:02:03.756 --> 00:02:06.126 align:start size:94% position:6%
one of your colleagues,
Senator Bob Corker,

00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:07.360 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
he is retiring.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.363 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it seems like
when Senators retire --

00:02:10.363 --> 00:02:11.564 line:92%
don't take this personally --

00:02:11.564 --> 00:02:12.799 align:start size:94% position:6%
they get a little bit
more honest.

00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:15.201
-[ Laughs ]
-And so he used this opportunity

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:20.006
to say that Donald Trump,
you know, is adult daycare,

00:02:20.006 --> 00:02:21.708 line:92%
requiring of adult daycare,

00:02:21.708 --> 00:02:23.243 align:start size:94% position:6%
that he might be starting
World War III.

00:02:23.243 --> 00:02:25.645
When you hear those comments,
do you sort of nod your head

00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:27.180 align:start size:94% position:6%
and say, "Oh,
that's what we're thinking

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.382
on this side of the aisle,"
or are you sort of shocked

00:02:29.382 --> 00:02:31.684 align:start size:94% position:6%
that it's even happening?
-Well, first of all,

00:02:31.684 --> 00:02:33.486 size:75% position:38%
this isn't new to --
I mean, this is one

00:02:33.486 --> 00:02:34.854 align:start size:94% position:6%
of the worst kept secrets
in Washington,

00:02:34.854 --> 00:02:38.158 align:start size:94% position:6%
that Republicans and Democrats
are very worried about

00:02:38.158 --> 00:02:40.160 align:start size:94% position:6%
the person that's...
-Mm-hmm.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:41.394 line:92%
-...sitting in the White House.

00:02:41.394 --> 00:02:42.829 align:start size:94% position:6%
And literally,
during the confirma--

00:02:42.829 --> 00:02:47.433
I mean, I voted against most
of Donald Trump's appointees.

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:50.503 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, from --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:55.108 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, Jeff Sessions,
Pruitt, DeVos.

00:02:55.108 --> 00:02:57.343 align:start size:94% position:6%
These are people that had me
really concerned.

00:02:57.343 --> 00:02:59.279 align:start size:94% position:6%
But you've got a guy like
General Mattis,

00:02:59.279 --> 00:03:01.814 size:81% position:41%
and the conversations
surrounding that were,

00:03:01.814 --> 00:03:03.917
"Let's make sure we put
the Secretary of Defense,

00:03:03.917 --> 00:03:07.587
"somebody that we can all
rely on to try to be a bulwark,

00:03:07.587 --> 00:03:10.590 align:start size:94% position:6%
a wall between
Donald Trump and disaster."

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:14.327
-It seems that's an unfair thing
to ask of the people

00:03:14.327 --> 00:03:16.696 align:start size:94% position:6%
that keep getting mentioned
as the ones

00:03:16.696 --> 00:03:18.097 align:start size:94% position:6%
who are supposed
to save us from danger.

00:03:18.097 --> 00:03:19.632
Because it seems like being
the Secretary of Defense

00:03:19.632 --> 00:03:21.968 align:start size:94% position:6%
or the Secretary of the State,
those jobs are hard enough

00:03:21.968 --> 00:03:24.237 align:start size:94% position:6%
without also having to keep
an eye on the President

00:03:24.237 --> 00:03:28.041 align:start size:94% position:6%
not, um...ruining everything
all the time.

00:03:28.041 --> 00:03:29.542 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:29.542 --> 00:03:34.214
-Look, and obviously this is a
comedy show, but this really --

00:03:34.214 --> 00:03:36.282 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Sometimes.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:36.282 --> 00:03:37.450 align:start size:94% position:6%
-On a good night.
-In and out.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:38.985 align:start size:94% position:6%
-On a good night.
On a good night.

00:03:38.985 --> 00:03:39.719 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yeah, yeah.

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:41.421 align:start size:94% position:6%
-But I'm very concerned
right now.

00:03:41.421 --> 00:03:45.592
I mean, we have crises all over
the planet Earth,

00:03:45.592 --> 00:03:46.292 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
serious things.

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:48.895 align:start size:94% position:6%
We've lost some soldiers
last week in Niger.

00:03:48.895 --> 00:03:53.166 align:start size:94% position:6%
And there are hot spots
from the Ukraine

00:03:53.166 --> 00:03:56.135 size:75% position:38% line:92%
to Syria to Nigeria.

00:03:56.135 --> 00:03:58.137 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, you can go through
the challenges.

00:03:58.137 --> 00:04:00.240 line:92%
And we're in a really bad spot.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.976 size:84% position:42%
Not only have we had
our Secretary of State,

00:04:02.976 --> 00:04:04.978 align:start size:94% position:6%
who should be
the leader of diplomacy,

00:04:04.978 --> 00:04:07.013 align:start size:94% position:6%
but you have an administration
who has been

00:04:07.013 --> 00:04:09.515 align:start size:94% position:6%
savagely cutting diplomacy,
even just funding

00:04:09.515 --> 00:04:12.452 align:start size:94% position:6%
the kind of things that help
keep regions stable.

00:04:12.452 --> 00:04:15.355 align:start size:94% position:6%
Donald Trump's willingness
to speak in a manner

00:04:15.355 --> 00:04:21.594
about nuclear war, that is,
to me, terrifying, frankly,

00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:24.631 align:start size:94% position:6%
about the consequences of
someone who decides

00:04:24.631 --> 00:04:26.199 align:start size:94% position:6%
to conduct foreign policy
that way.

00:04:26.199 --> 00:04:27.567 line:92%
We -- And, I mean, when we --

00:04:27.567 --> 00:04:30.336
I'm on the Foreign Relations
Committee with Senator Corker.

00:04:30.336 --> 00:04:32.639
I've been grateful
for Senator Corker's leadership

00:04:32.639 --> 00:04:35.742 align:start size:94% position:6%
in trying to keep us together
in a bipartisan way

00:04:35.742 --> 00:04:37.610 align:start size:94% position:6%
to push back against
the White House.

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:40.146 align:start size:94% position:6%
So we have work to do.
This is not --

00:04:40.146 --> 00:04:42.081 line:92%
This is a very serious reality,

00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:44.350 align:start size:94% position:6%
especially with
the important role

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:46.286 align:start size:94% position:6%
the United States has
been playing

00:04:46.286 --> 00:04:49.489 align:start size:94% position:6%
in stabilizing regions with
high instability,

00:04:49.489 --> 00:04:50.923 align:start size:94% position:6%
in dealing with
global terrorism.

00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:52.959 align:start size:94% position:6%
We've been a very good leader,
and right now,

00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:55.728
it seems that we're pulling back
and operating in a manner

00:04:55.728 --> 00:04:57.964 align:start size:94% position:6%
that has our allies --
I just recently met

00:04:57.964 --> 00:05:01.100 size:81% position:41%
with some ambassadors
from various countries

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:02.235 size:84% position:42% line:92%
that we're allied with.

00:05:02.235 --> 00:05:03.069 line:92%
This is a very perilous time,

00:05:03.069 --> 00:05:05.505
and all Americans, not just
should be joking about it,

00:05:05.505 --> 00:05:09.909
but we should be concerned
where -- in the state we are.

00:05:09.909 --> 00:05:11.244 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I don't think patriotism,
right?

00:05:11.244 --> 00:05:14.147
I would like to see people
on both sides of the aisle,

00:05:14.147 --> 00:05:16.382 line:92%
not just talking about this.

00:05:16.382 --> 00:05:19.952
I think we all need to be doing
more about this.

